1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,303
Two graphics cards with the exact same chip ran the same game.

2
00:00:04,583 --> 00:00:10,991
One averaged 66 frames a second, the other managed 27, with dips down to 9.

3
00:00:11,271 --> 00:00:20,982
The only difference was the amount of video memory on the card, and by the end of this video you'll know exactly why that happens, and how much you actually need.

4
00:00:21,732 --> 00:00:33,087
The cards were the RTX 5060 Ti, one with 8 gigabytes of VRAM and one with 16, tested by TechSpot in Dragon Age: The Veilguard.

5
00:00:33,367 --> 00:00:39,936
VRAM is memory soldered right around the graphics chip, separate from the RAM your processor uses.

6
00:00:40,216 --> 00:00:50,003
On this card it's GDDR7, a type of memory built for huge, wide transfers, connected over a 128 bit bus.

7
00:00:50,283 --> 00:00:52,825
It exists because a GPU is hungry.

8
00:00:53,105 --> 00:00:58,429
This card can read its own memory at about 448 gigabytes a second.

9
00:00:58,709 --> 00:01:04,163
Everything the GPU needs to draw a frame has to sit in that memory, ready to grab.

10
00:01:04,913 --> 00:01:07,522
Think of VRAM as a suitcase, so let's pack it.

11
00:01:07,802 --> 00:01:13,459
The biggest item by far is textures, the images wrapped over every surface in the game.

12
00:01:13,739 --> 00:01:22,214
A single detailed texture, 4096 pixels on each side, would take 64 megabytes if it were stored raw.

13
00:01:22,494 --> 00:01:30,599
Games compress it with a format called BC7, which uses one byte per pixel, so it drops to 16 megabytes.

14
00:01:30,879 --> 00:01:36,219
Then the game adds smaller copies for distant objects, which costs about a third more.

15
00:01:36,499 --> 00:01:42,549
And one material often uses several of these textures at once, for color, bumps and shine.

16
00:01:42,829 --> 00:01:48,738
Ten materials like that, with three textures each, is already over 600 megabytes.

17
00:01:49,488 --> 00:01:59,544
Now multiply that by every rock, wall, face and weapon in view, and in the areas nearby, because the game loads them before you turn around.

18
00:01:59,824 --> 00:02:02,069
After textures come the frame buffers.

19
00:02:02,349 --> 00:02:14,224
One 1440p image is only about 15 megabytes, but modern games keep many of them: depth, motion, lighting, and older frames for upscaling.

20
00:02:14,504 --> 00:02:24,765
Add the 3D models, the shader programs, and with ray tracing on, a whole extra map of the scene's geometry so light rays can find what they hit.

21
00:02:25,045 --> 00:02:27,806
Frame generation needs its own room too.

22
00:02:28,556 --> 00:02:31,963
So why does this suddenly matter more than a few years ago?

23
00:02:32,243 --> 00:02:35,171
Because big games are built for the current consoles.

24
00:02:35,451 --> 00:02:42,073
A PlayStation 5 has 16 gigabytes of fast memory, shared between its processor and graphics.

25
00:02:42,353 --> 00:02:52,242
Developers plan their textures and effects around that budget, and the PC version's high settings often ask for more than 8 gigabytes on the card alone.

26
00:02:52,522 --> 00:03:02,790
When PC Gamer tested recent games this year, the 16 gigabyte cards were using around 9 to 11, while the 8 gigabyte cards were nearly full.

27
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:05,869
Here's what happens when the suitcase is full.

28
00:03:06,149 --> 00:03:07,534
The game doesn't crash.

29
00:03:07,814 --> 00:03:12,407
The driver moves some of the data out to your system RAM instead.

30
00:03:12,687 --> 00:03:19,585
Now, whenever the GPU needs one of those textures, it has to fetch it across the PCI Express slot.

31
00:03:19,865 --> 00:03:31,868
On the RTX 5060 Ti, which only wires up 8 lanes, a PCIe 4 slot gives about 32 gigabytes a second, counting both directions.

32
00:03:32,148 --> 00:03:35,731
Compare that with 448 inside the card.

33
00:03:36,011 --> 00:03:45,785
And the system RAM on the other end is slower too: dual channel DDR5 at 6000 peaks around 96 gigabytes a second.

34
00:03:46,065 --> 00:03:50,210
It's like a chef whose ingredients are suddenly in a shop down the street.

35
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:56,315
That's why running out of VRAM often shows up as stutter rather than a lower average.

36
00:03:56,595 --> 00:03:58,721
Most frames still find what they need.

37
00:03:59,001 --> 00:04:05,265
But every so often a frame has to wait for a texture to arrive, and that frame takes far longer.

38
00:04:05,545 --> 00:04:10,047
At 60 frames a second, each frame gets about 16 milliseconds.

39
00:04:10,327 --> 00:04:17,949
A frame that waits two or three times as long is a hitch you can feel, even if the counter in the corner barely moves.

40
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:24,359
Reviewers catch this with a number called the 1 percent low, the frame rate of the slowest frames.

41
00:04:24,639 --> 00:04:33,999
In PC Gamer's Cyberpunk test at 1080p with medium ray tracing, both cards averaged 91 frames a second.

42
00:04:34,279 --> 00:04:40,962
But the 1 percent lows were 63 on the 16 gigabyte card and only 44 on the 8.

43
00:04:41,712 --> 00:04:44,874
Your motherboard slot changes how bad the fall is.

44
00:04:45,154 --> 00:04:52,520
That 27 frames a second at the start was on an older PCIe 3 slot, which halves the bandwidth again.

45
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:58,224
On PCIe 4, the 8 gigabyte card's 1 percent lows more than doubled.

46
00:04:58,504 --> 00:05:02,761
On PCIe 5, its average rose another 12 percent.

47
00:05:03,041 --> 00:05:07,998
But even there, the 16 gigabyte card was still 68 percent faster.

48
00:05:08,278 --> 00:05:10,183
A faster slot softens the hit.

49
00:05:10,463 --> 00:05:12,642
It can't replace memory on the card.

50
00:05:13,392 --> 00:05:15,145
It isn't the same in every game.

51
00:05:15,425 --> 00:05:25,368
In The Last of Us Part 2, the 8 gigabyte card on PCIe 5 averaged 92 frames a second, level with the 16 gigabyte card.

52
00:05:25,648 --> 00:05:35,414
In other games tested the gap was huge, and Indiana Jones and Monster Hunter Wilds struggled on 8 gigabytes no matter which slot was used.

53
00:05:35,694 --> 00:05:43,077
What decides it is mostly four settings: texture quality, resolution, ray tracing and frame generation.

54
00:05:43,357 --> 00:05:51,260
Textures are usually the biggest single user, so dropping texture quality one step is the quickest way to free memory.

55
00:05:52,010 --> 00:05:53,451
So how much do you need?

56
00:05:53,731 --> 00:06:02,909
PC Gamer's verdict was that 8 gigabytes is still enough for gaming in 2026, with caveats, and the caveats are the point.

57
00:06:03,189 --> 00:06:14,471
If you already have an 8 gigabyte card, play at 1080p, keep textures at high instead of ultra, and go easy on ray tracing and frame generation.

58
00:06:14,751 --> 00:06:25,478
If you're buying a new card to keep for years, 12 gigabytes is a sensible floor for 1440p, and 16 gives you room as games grow.

59
00:06:25,758 --> 00:06:33,918
And check your slot: an 8 lane card in an older PCIe 3 board gets hit twice as hard when memory runs out.

60
00:06:34,668 --> 00:06:36,797
You can check your own card right now.

61
00:06:37,077 --> 00:06:44,729
Open Task Manager, go to Performance, pick your GPU, and watch dedicated GPU memory while you play.

62
00:06:45,009 --> 00:06:49,554
If it sits right at the limit and your game stutters, you've found your bottleneck.

63
00:06:49,834 --> 00:06:57,846
Remember the suitcase: when it's full, the GPU waits on the shop down the street, and no amount of raw speed fixes that.

64
00:06:58,126 --> 00:07:03,830
Next time, we're opening the case and cleaning a PC properly without breaking anything.

65
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:05,791
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

